  <Callout title="Acadia Tourism Funnel" type="passion">
    Acadia National Park logged 3.96 million recreation visits in 2024 (NPS), with the vast majority funneling through Bar Harbor in the summer. For a cannabis operator, the strategic question is location along that funnel: a Trenton storefront captures cars crossing the bridge, a Main Street location captures foot traffic already in Bar Harbor, and a store out on ME-3 toward the park entrance captures the final-mile flow. Today the funnel is covered by licensed players: The Meristem holds the island's only active adult-use license, Snare Creek Farms and The Happier Choice hold the Trenton medical corridor, and 420 Mules serves island medical patients by delivery. The gap — an active adult-use license in Bar Harbor proper, where voters rejected retail ordinances twice in 2022 — is the open commercial question.
  </Callout>

  <section>
    <h2>Bar Harbor's Market Context</h2>
    <p>Bar Harbor's year-round population — 5,089 at the 2020 Census and an estimated 5,352 in 2025 — is dwarfed by the seasonal flow: Acadia is one of the ten most-visited national parks in the United States, and Bar Harbor is the gateway community for nearly all of that traffic. The town's compact downtown and walkable Main Street make it an unusually strong cannabis retail market during the May–October season.</p>
    <p>For operators, Bar Harbor is a different market from Ellsworth about 22 miles west (roughly 40 minutes on US-3/US-1). Ellsworth functions as a year-round regional hub for Hancock County; Bar Harbor is a compressed, high-intensity seasonal market. The town has NOT authorized adult-use retail — voters rejected citizen-initiated retail ordinances in June 2022 and November 2022 — and the OCP roster (2026-07-01) shows no active adult-use store license in Bar Harbor itself. The island's licensed rec sales happen at The Meristem in Southwest Harbor, while the town's own cannabis presence is the medical side (420 Mules delivery). Across the bridge, Trenton's Snare Creek and The Happier Choice serve the mainland side of the same customer base with medical access, and together the locations form the Mount Desert Island / Acadia cannabis corridor.</p>
    <p>Ellsworth's medical cluster gives patients a mainland fallback: Curaleaf Ellsworth (formerly Maine Organic Therapy, OCP registered dispensary DSP105) and Main Street Medical Caregivers, plus the River Rise adult-use store in nearby Bucksport (159 US Route 1, active per the OCP roster). For tourists, the practical question is whether to stop in Trenton (closer to the highway) or push on to The Meristem on the island (closer to where most visitors stay). Many visitors hit both, just as they hit multiple restaurants and shops on the same trip.</p>
  </section>

